\A{folderstore} The folder store

This appendix documents the folders and files of the KiTTY++ folder
store: where it is, the line format of a session file, the passwords in
it and the other files the store keeps. It is written for
administrators who generate session files, keep them in Git or inspect
them.

The folder store is in use when \c{kitty.ini} holds
\c{[KiTTY] savemode=dir}. It is the default of \c{kitty_portable.exe},
which writes that key into its \c{kitty.ini} on its first start. Without
it KiTTY++ keeps its settings in the registry, under
\c{HKEY_CURRENT_USER\\Software\\kapper.net\\KiTTY}. klink, kscp and
ksftp from the same folder read the same store. The settings that shape
the store are on the Storage & Backup panel, \k{kitty-cfg-storage}.

\H{folderstore-where} Where the store is

KiTTY++ takes the first of these files that exists as its \c{kitty.ini}:

\n the file \c{%KITTY_INI_FILE%} points to

\n \c{kitty.ini} in the folder of the program

\n \c{%APPDATA%\\KiTTY\\kitty.ini} (not looked at by the portable build)

Every key below is in the \c{[KiTTY]} section. The KiTTY.ini leaf shows
the file in use, \k{kitty-cfg-ini-view}.

klink, kscp, ksftp and kageant also take the folder store when
\c{kitty.ini} holds no \c{savemode} key at all but a \c{Sessions} folder
or a \c{KiTTYState} file sits beside it.

Four places follow from \c{kitty.ini}:

\b The \e{configuration directory} is \c{configdir}: an absolute path,
or a path below the folder of the program. A \c{configdir} that does not
exist counts as not set. Without one, the configuration directory is the
folder of the program.

\b The \e{session folder} is \c{sessions} when it is set. Windows
variables such as \c{%USERPROFILE%} are expanded in it. A value of the
form \c{X:\\...} is an absolute path. Every other value, one starting
with \c{\\} included, is taken below the folder of the program, so a UNC
path does not work here. Without \c{sessions}, the session folder is
\c{Sessions} in the configuration directory. kitty.exe creates a missing
session folder.

\b The \e{store root} is the parent of the session folder when the last
component of the session folder is \c{Sessions} (in any case). Otherwise
the store root is the session folder itself.

\b The \e{host-key folder} is \c{sshhostkeys}, resolved like
\c{sessions}. Without it, the host-key folder is \c{SshHostKeys} in the
store root.

\H{folderstore-layout} The layout

In the default layout the session folder is \c{Sessions} in the
configuration directory, so the store root and the configuration
directory are one folder:

\c <configuration directory>\
\c     kitty.ini        global options, when it lives here
\c     KiTTYState       remembered state, key=value lines
\c     Jumplist         recent sessions, one per line
\c     PUTTY.RND        random seed
\c     Sessions\        session files, folders as directories
\c     SshHostKeys\     accepted host keys, one file per key
\c     SshHostCAs\      trusted host certificate authorities
\c     Security\        the master password's check material
\c     Proxies\         named proxy definitions
\c     Launcher\        the launcher's menu entries
\c     Backups\         dated copies of the configuration directory
\c     TermEnvHint\     one mark per host
\c     WindowPos\       terminal window positions

The two roots part when \c{sessions} points somewhere else:

\b In the \e{store root}: \c{KiTTYState}, \c{Jumplist}, \c{PUTTY.RND},
\c{SshHostKeys} (unless \c{sshhostkeys} is set), \c{SshHostCAs},
\c{Security}, \c{TermEnvHint} and \c{WindowPos}.

\b In the \e{configuration directory}: \c{Proxies}, \c{Launcher} and
\c{Backups}.

With \c{sessions=D:\\ops\\mysess}, for example, \c{KiTTYState} and the
\c{Security} folder are kept inside \c{D:\\ops\\mysess}, while
\c{Proxies} stays in the configuration directory. They are never listed
as sessions. A tool that regenerates the session folder must leave them
in place.

Host-key files, the files in \c{Security}, \c{TermEnvHint} and
\c{WindowPos} are written whole: a temporary file first, moved over the
old one once it is complete. Session files, \c{KiTTYState}, \c{Jumplist} and the files in
\c{Proxies} and \c{SshHostCAs} are written over in place.

\H{folderstore-sessions} Session folders and file names

\b Each saved session is one file. The session \c{Linux\\web\\srv01} is
the file \c{Linux\\web\\srv01} below the session folder, with the
session file extension appended. Each directory is one folder level, up
to 24 levels deep.

\b A folder exists through its sessions. A directory without a session
file in it is not listed. When KiTTY++ deletes or moves a session, it
removes the directories this leaves empty, up to the session folder. A
directory that still holds any file, a \c{.gitkeep} for example, stays.

\b Names that start with a dot are never looked at: \c{.git},
\c{.gitignore}, \c{.gitkeep}. A junction or a symbolic link to a
directory is not followed. A file with a reparse point, such as a
placeholder of a synced folder, is read like any other file.

\b A file is a session file when one of its lines starts with
\c{HostName} or \c{Protocol}, followed directly by \c{=} or \c{\\}. The
key is case-sensitive and must open the line: no space in front of it,
no byte-order mark in front of the first line. Other files beside the
sessions, a \c{README.md} or a log, are not listed.

\b File and folder names are in the ANSI code page of Windows. A file or
folder whose name does not fit it is marked in the session list, which
offers a rename. A folder of that kind is not looked into.

\b \c{fileextension} is the \e{session file extension} (\q{Session File
Extension} on \k{kitty-cfg-storage}): none by default, a dot put in
front when it is missing. It ends every session file name. The list
shows the names without it. An extension holding a control character or
one of \c{\\ / : * ? " < > |} is not used.

\S{folderstore-escape} How a session name becomes a file name

Each component of the session's path is escaped on its own:

\b \c{%}, \c{\\}, \c{/}, \c{:}, \c{*}, \c{?}, \c{"}, \c{<}, \c{>},
\c{|}, control characters and DEL become \c{%} followed by two uppercase
hex digits. The session \c{core/sw01} is the file \c{core%2Fsw01}.

\b A leading dot becomes \c{%2E}. A trailing dot becomes \c{%2E}, a
trailing space \c{%20}.

\b A Windows device name (\c{CON}, \c{PRN}, \c{AUX}, \c{NUL},
\c{COM1} to \c{COM9}, \c{LPT1} to \c{LPT9}), also followed by an
extension, has its first character escaped: \c{%43ON}, \c{%63on.txt}.

\b Everything else stays as it is: spaces inside a name, \c{=}, \c{#},
bytes above 127. \c{Default Settings} is the file \c{Default Settings}
with a real space.

When reading, KiTTY++ decodes \c{%} followed by two hex digits in either
case. A \c{%} not followed by two hex digits stays as it is. A file
spelt another way, with \c{%20} for a space or lower-case hex digits for
example, is listed under its decoded name and saved in place under its
own file name. When two files decode to the same session, one of them is
the session and the session list marks the others.

Older layouts are read too:

\b A session file without the session file extension. Its next save
writes the file with the extension and removes the old one. When both
files exist, a save of that session is refused.

\b A file at the top of the session folder whose name holds an escaped
\c{\\}, such as \c{Linux%5Cweb01}: the session \c{Linux\\web01} of the
layout before session names became paths. It is read while it exists.
Its next save moves it to \c{Linux\\web01}. Below the top level a
file name holding an escaped \c{\\} is not listed.

Without an extension a session and a folder can need the same name on
disk: the session \c{Linux} is the file \c{Linux}, the folder \c{Linux}
the directory \c{Linux}. A save, a move or a new folder that meets the
other one is refused.

The directory decides the folder of a session. A \c{Folder} value in a
file inside a directory is ignored. The next save writes the matching
value. Session folders as the configuration window shows them:
\k{kitty-cfg-folders}.

\H{folderstore-format} The session file format

A session file is text, one setting per line. Two line forms are read:

\c HostName=web01.example.com
\c HostName\web01.example.com\

The first is KiTTY++'s own form. The second is the form old KiTTY wrote
in its portable store and in \c{.ktx} files.

\b The form is decided line by line: the first \c{=} or \c{\\} in the
line ends the key. A key never holds either character. In the first
form the value may hold \c{=}: \c{Key=a=b} is the value \c{a=b}. In the
second form one trailing \c{\\} is removed. A line without it, such as
\c{Key\\0}, reads the same.

\b A line with neither \c{=} nor \c{\\} is not a setting. It is kept as
it stands, at its place, through every save. A blank line is kept the
same way. This is how a comment goes into a session file:
\c{# generated from the inventory} is one, as long as it holds neither
\c{=} nor \c{\\}.

\b The key is the text before the delimiter, as it stands. Keys are
case-sensitive and no space is removed: \c{HostName = x} is the key
\c{HostName} followed by a space, with a value starting with a space.

\b Values are escaped when written: control characters, DEL, \c{%},
\c{\\}, \c{/}, \c{:}, \c{*}, \c{?}, \c{"}, \c{<}, \c{>} and \c{|}
become \c{%} followed by two uppercase hex digits. A key file is
\c{PublicKeyFile=D:%5Ckeys%5Cid.ppk}. A line break inside a value is
\c{%0A}. \c{=} and spaces are not escaped. When reading, \c{%} followed
by two hex digits is decoded in either case. Any other \c{%} stays as
it is.

\b Numbers and switches are decimal text: \c{PortNumber=2222},
\c{AgentFwd=1}.

\b A key on two lines takes the value of the later line. A save keeps
one line for it, at the place of the first.

\b Lines may end in LF or CRLF. A save keeps the ending of each line. A
line it adds takes the ending of the first line of the file. A new file
is written with LF. A last line without a line ending gets one.

\b A line may be of any length.

\b A byte-order mark is not removed. It becomes part of the first key.

A save works on the file as it is on disk:

\b It reads the file first and writes it whole. Lines it does not set -
keys of other tools, comments, blank lines - keep their place.

\b A line whose value did not change is written back exactly as it was
read, in its own form, spacing included. A Git diff after a settings
change shows the changed lines only.

\b A line whose value changed is replaced at its place, in the form
\c{Key=value}.

\b A key the file did not have goes to the end. KiTTY++ writes every
setting it has, so a file of three lines holds every setting after its
first save from KiTTY++.

\b Keys of retired settings are removed: \c{SaveWindowPos},
\c{OSC52WarnBeforeClipboardSync}, \c{CheckUpdateStartup},
\c{ScriptFileName}, \c{Present}, \c{DisableBottomButtons} and
\c{Notes}. Where the setting has a successor that means the same, the
old key is read while the new one is missing.

\b KiTTY++ reads the file when a save starts and writes it over when
the save ends. An edit made to the same file in between is lost.

\H{folderstore-passwords} Passwords in a session file

Two keys hold secrets: \c{Password} (the auto-login password) and
\c{ProxyPassword}. A marker at the start of the value decides how it is
read, whatever store holds it. In a file the \c{:} of a marker is
escaped like any other, so a stored value starts with \c{MPW2%3A},
\c{DPAPI1%3A} and so on. A \c{/} of the base64 text appears as
\c{%2F}. Both spellings are read.

\b \c{MPW2:<salt>.<payload>}: the master password, self-contained. The
base64 text of the 16-byte Argon2id salt, a dot, then the encrypted
value (AES-256-CBC with an HMAC). The master password alone opens it, on
any PC and in any store. This is what KiTTY++ writes under the
master-password policy.

\b \c{MPW1:<payload>}: the master password, with the salt kept in the
store's \c{Security\\MasterPwSalt}. KiTTY++ reads this form but no
longer writes it. It opens only together with the \c{Security} folder of
the store that wrote it.

\b \c{DPAPI1:<base64>}: Windows DPAPI. Only this Windows account on this
PC opens it.

\b \c{PLAIN:<password>}: a password in clear text, for files a
provisioning script writes. The rest of the value is taken literally.
KiTTY++ never writes this marker.

\b A value without a marker is taken as the password itself, in clear
text. In a file with at least one line in the \c{Key\\value\\} form, a
\c{Password} value that has the shape of old KiTTY's encrypted form is
decoded as one when it decodes. In a file of \c{Key=value} lines alone
it stays clear text.

What a save writes is set by \c{PortablePasswordProtection} (\q{Passwords
protected with} on \k{kitty-cfg-storage}):

\b \c{master} (the default): \c{MPW2}. The first password saved sets up
the master password. From a script, \c{-masterpwfile} supplies it,
\k{using-cmdline-masterpwfile}.

\b \c{dpapi}: \c{DPAPI1}. No master password is set up.

\b \c{legacy}: the password in clear text, without a marker.

Reading does not depend on the setting: each value is read by its own
marker. A save keeps a stored line in these cases:

\b The password did not change and the stored value is already in the
form this save would write. A new wrap would change the line on every
save.

\b The stored value could not be opened here, a \c{DPAPI1} value from
another PC for example. It is written back as it was.

\b The master password is locked: its setup or unlock was cancelled, or
the password typed was wrong. KiTTY++ reports the kept line.

\b The stored value is unmarked or \c{PLAIN:} while the save would
replace it with a protected one. KiTTY++ puts up a question first. No
keeps the stored line.
\c{WarnLegacyPasswordUpgrade=no} skips the question and protects the
password. klink, kscp and ksftp keep the stored line without a question.

\H{folderstore-security} Security: the master password's check material

The \c{Security} folder in the store root holds two files of one line
each. They are written together, the first time a master password is
set up:

\b \c{MasterPwSalt}: the base64 text of the 16 random bytes from which,
with the master password, Argon2id derives the key.

\b \c{MasterPwVerifier}: a fixed text encrypted under that key, in the
\c{MPW1:} form. It proves that a typed master password is the right
one.

Neither file holds a password or the key. They are check material. The
salt is a public random input. The verifier only confirms a password
typed.

\b When kitty.exe opens its configuration window at start, KiTTY++ puts
up the master-password prompt once if \c{MasterPwVerifier} exists and
\c{PortablePasswordProtection} is \c{master}. The launcher does the same
when it starts. Under \c{dpapi} or \c{legacy} no prompt comes at start:
a value still \c{MPW2} brings the prompt when it is read.

\b Without the \c{Security} folder, \c{MPW2} values still open. Each
carries its own salt. The master password typed at the prompt is checked
against the value itself. No prompt comes at start. Under the
master-password policy, the next password a save protects sets up a
master password anew, with a new salt and verifier. Values written before keep their own salt and need the
password they were written with.

\b \c{MPW1} values cannot be opened without the \c{Security} folder of
the store that wrote them.

\b \c{Security} copied from another store makes that store's master
password the one this store checks at start and uses for new values.
Values written before keep their own salt and still need their own
password. To share one master password between several portable copies,
copy one \c{Security} folder into each before passwords are saved
there.

\b A store whose passwords were wrapped by an earlier version may have
no \c{Security} folder, its salt having been kept in the registry. At
start KiTTY++ copies the salt and verifier from the registry into the
store, once: when the store holds a master-password value, has no
\c{MasterPwSalt} of its own and the registry still holds one.

\H{folderstore-proxies} Proxies: named proxy definitions

\c{Proxies} in the configuration directory holds one file per named
proxy definition (\k{kitty-cfg-named-proxies}). The folder is made by
the first definition saved.

\b The file name is the definition's name, escaped the way old KiTTY
escaped it: space, \c{\\}, \c{*}, \c{?}, \c{:}, \c{/}, \c{"}, \c{<},
\c{>}, \c{|}, \c{%}, control characters, bytes above 126 and a leading
dot become \c{%} followed by two uppercase hex digits. Files called
\c{None} or \c{Default} are not taken as definitions.

\b The readers take only lines in the form \c{Key\\value\\}. A line
\c{Key=value} is not read. The value is escaped as in a session file. Use uppercase hex digits here: lower-case ones are
not decoded correctly.

\b A save reads the file first, keeps lines it does not set (comments,
keys of other tools) at their place, keeps unchanged lines as they were
written and writes changed and new lines in the \c{Key\\value\\} form.

The keys:

\b \c{ProxyMethod}: \c{0} none, \c{1} SOCKS 4, \c{2} SOCKS 5, \c{3}
HTTP, \c{4} Telnet, \c{5} local command, \c{6} SSH port forwarding,
\c{7} SSH command, \c{8} SSH subsystem. Any other number is none.

\b \c{ProxyHost}, \c{ProxyPort}, \c{ProxyUsername}.

\b \c{ProxyPassword}: protected as a session's password, with the same
markers (\k{folderstore-passwords}). An unchanged password keeps its
line.

\b \c{ProxyExcludeList}: destinations that bypass the proxy.

\b \c{ProxyDNS}: \c{0} no, \c{1} auto, \c{2} yes.

\b \c{ProxyLocalhost}: \c{1} sends connections to \c{localhost} through
the proxy too.

\b \c{ProxyTelnetCommand}: the command to send.

\b \c{ProxyLogToTerm}: diagnostics in the terminal, \c{0} on, \c{1}
off, \c{2} auto.

\b \c{ProxyHostIs}: \c{0} or \c{1}, how the host field is read. A save
leaves the line out when the definition follows the global default.

A session refers to a definition by its name, in its own file:
\c{ProxySelection=<name>}, escaped as any value.
\c{- Session defined proxy -} (the default) uses the session's own proxy
settings. \c{- No proxy -} uses none (\k{kitty-cfg-proxy-override}). A name without a
definition falls back to the session's own proxy settings when the
session has some, else to no proxy.

\H{folderstore-hostkeys} SshHostKeys and SshHostCAs

The host-key folder holds one file per accepted host key.

\b The file name is \c{<key type>@<port>:<host>}. The host is escaped
as PuTTY escapes it in the registry: space, \c{\\}, \c{*}, \c{?},
\c{%}, control characters, bytes above 126 and a leading dot become
\c{%} followed by two uppercase hex digits. The whole name is then
escaped as a session value, so \c{:} becomes \c{%3A} and a \c{%} from
the first step becomes \c{%25}. The host-key file extension is appended last.

\b The file holds the key in the text form PuTTY keeps under
\c{SshHostKeys} in the registry, followed by a line ending. A stored
key matches only when this text is exactly the key the server presents.

\b Two more files go with each key, the same name with an ending. The
one ending in \c{%3Afirst} is written when the key is first stored. The
one ending in \c{%3Awhen} is written each time the key is stored. Each holds a local time,
\c{YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss}. They are information for the Host keys panel
(\k{kitty-cfg-host-keys}). Nothing decides on them. Deleting a key there
deletes all three files.

An example, for an Ed25519 key of \c{web01.example.com} on port 22:

\c ssh-ed25519@22%3Aweb01.example.com
\c ssh-ed25519@22%3Aweb01.example.com%3Afirst
\c ssh-ed25519@22%3Aweb01.example.com%3Awhen

\c{hostkeyextension} (\q{Host Key File Extension} on
\k{kitty-cfg-storage}) is an ending on every host-key file name: none by
default, a dot put in front when it is missing. A file without the
ending is renamed to carry it when its host is first looked up. Where
both names exist, the one with the ending is read.
\c{hostkeyextensionapplied} in \c{kitty.ini} records the ending last
applied to the files.

\c{SshHostCAs} in the store root holds one file per trusted host
certificate authority (\k{kitty-cfg-host-cas}). The file name is the
CA's name, escaped as a session value. The file holds \c{Key=value}
lines: \c{PublicKey} (the CA key, base64), \c{Validity} (the expression
of hosts the CA is trusted for, percent-encoded) and
\c{PermitRSASHA1}, \c{PermitRSASHA256}, \c{PermitRSASHA512} (\c{0} or
\c{1}). Each save writes the file anew.

\H{folderstore-state} KiTTYState and the other state files

\c{KiTTYState} in the store root holds what KiTTY++ remembers between
runs, in the line format of a session file. Numbers are decimal text.

\b \c{LastSession}, \c{LastFolder}: the session and the folder the
configuration window last loaded. \c{LastFolder=Default} is the root.

\b \c{ManageFolder}, \c{ManageSession}, \c{ManageTag}: where Manage
Sessions was left.

\b \c{ShowForeignSessions}: \c{1} lists the sessions of the old KiTTY
and PuTTY registry hives as well.

\b \c{ForeignSessionsNotice}: the notice about those sessions still to
show, as bits: \c{1} at start, \c{2} under the session list.

\b \c{SessionArrangeOffered}: \c{1} once Arrange has been offered.

\b \c{UpdateLatest}: the newest version the update check found.
\c{UpdateLatestBeta}: \c{1} when that version is a beta.

KiTTY++ reads the file each time it needs a value. To set one, it reads
the file, changes that line and writes the file over. A missing
\c{KiTTYState} is no error: each value then has its default, so the
Arrange offer comes again where it applies.

The other state files:

\b \c{Jumplist} (store root): the recent sessions of the jump list, one
session name per line, escaped as a session value, the newest first.
Each change drops the names whose session no longer exists.

\b \c{PUTTY.RND} (store root): the random seed. Binary, rewritten by
KiTTY++.

\b \c{TermEnvHint} (store root): one file per host, holding \c{1}, the
file name escaped as a session value. It marks that the notice
about a host refusing \c{COLORTERM} or \c{TERM_PROGRAM} was shown.

\b \c{WindowPos} (store root): where terminal windows were left, one
file per monitor layout.

\b \c{Launcher} (configuration directory): the launcher's menu, one file
per entry, folders as directories. KiTTY++ deletes the folder and writes
it anew from the session list.

\H{folderstore-backups} Backups

KiTTY++ backs up the configuration directory into its \c{Backups}
folder:

\b \c{Backups\\kitty-portable-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS}: a dated copy, local
time.

\b \c{Backups\\kitty-portable-latest}: the same copy again, replaced
each time.

\b \c{portablebackupcount} is the number of dated copies kept: 5 by
default, 50 at most. 0 turns the backup off. After each backup the
oldest dated copies beyond that number are deleted.

A copy holds \c{kitty.ini} (taken from wherever it lives) and everything
in the configuration directory except, at its top level, the
\c{Backups} folder and files ending in \c{.exe}, \c{.dll}, \c{.log} or
\c{.dmp}. A session folder or host-key folder set outside the
configuration directory (\c{sessions}, \c{sshhostkeys}) is not in the
copy. A copy that fails part-way is deleted. The older copies are then
left as they are. Without a configuration file (\c{conf=no}) no
backup is written.

A backup is written:

\b When the configuration window is committed with \q{Open} or
\q{Change Settings} is applied, if the store has changed since the last
backup: a session saved or deleted, a host key stored or deleted, a
named proxy saved, the session files renamed to a new extension.

\b Before the configuration window overwrites or deletes a session,
whether or not anything changed.

\b On \c{/savereg}, at once.

To restore, close every KiTTY++ program and copy the contents of a
backup back into the configuration directory.

\H{folderstore-edits} Editing the store while KiTTY++ runs

Nothing watches the store. A change made by hand counts when KiTTY++
next reads the file in question:

\b A session file is read each time the session is loaded. A session
started by name (\c{kitty.exe -load Linux\\web01}) always takes the file
as it is. The configuration window reads the session list when it
opens. kageant reads it each time its tray menu opens.

\b \c{KiTTYState} is read each time a value is needed.

\b A host-key file is read at each connection to its host, so a key
added or deleted by hand counts at the next connection.

\b A named proxy's file is read when a session that uses it connects.

\b The \c{Security} files are read the first time a process needs the
master password. A process that has unlocked it keeps the key: a changed
\c{Security} folder counts in a KiTTY++ started afterwards.

\b \c{kitty.ini} is read when KiTTY++ starts.

KiTTY++ writes a session file, \c{KiTTYState} or a proxy file over in
place, from what it read a moment before. An edit saved to the same file
in that moment is lost.

\H{folderstore-ktx} .ktx export files

A \c{.ktx} file is one session outside the store: what Application >
Migration exports (\k{kitty-cfg-migration}), \c{-exportall} writes and
\c{-importdir} reads.

\b An export writes one flat file per session into the folder chosen.
Its name is the whole session name, folder path included, escaped as one
component, so \c{Linux\\web01} is \c{Linux%5Cweb01.ktx}. The ending is
the session file extension, \c{.ktx} when none is set. Default Settings
is not exported.

\b The lines are in the \c{Key\\value\\} form, with the same value
escape as a session file. Reading takes both forms.

\b The passwords are wrapped for transport, not for the store: with the
export's own password (at least five characters) as \c{MPW2}, opened on
any PC with that password, or with DPAPI when \q{this PC only} is
chosen. An export never sets up a master password in the store.

\b The named proxy definitions go into a \c{Proxies} folder beside the
exported files, in the format of \k{folderstore-proxies}.

\b An import saves each file as an ordinary session. The passwords are
then protected for the destination store, a \c{PLAIN:} value included.

\b Each line of a \c{.ktx} file must be empty, have its first \c{=}
before any \c{\\}, or end in \c{\\}. Any other line, a comment or
\c{Key\\0} without the closing \c{\\} included, marks the encrypted line
form of older KiTTY versions. The reader decodes it as one.

\q{Make a portable copy} on the Migration panel writes the copy's
session files through the export as well, but in the \c{Key=value} form
with LF, as a session file of the store.
